// A PROJECTION of a `const` item dangles exactly as the bare borrow does, and
// closing only the bare form left this open.
//
// A const is materialised into the FRAME at each use. `&K` is caught in
// `prov_of`'s AddrOf arm; `&K.a`, `&A[0]` and `&K.i.a` never reach that arm —
// they arrive at `value_local_root`'s VarRef terminal, which required
// `var_has(...)` and so fell straight through, because a const is not a tracked
// local. The same `frame_consts` set now answers at both places.
//
// MEASURED on the build that had only the bare form closed, with the clobber
// constant VARIED so a lucky-stable read cannot pass for a correct one:
// `return &A[0];` gave exit 71 for stomp 71 and exit 5 for stomp 5 — the second
// row is the trap, since 5 is also the correct answer.
//
// ⚠ The admit twin `pass/bc_static_item_return_ref_admit` covers the STATIC
// projection, and it is not symmetry: a `static` has real storage and sema
// rewrites its read to `Deref(VarRef "__static_addr:<sym>")`, so it never
// reaches this terminal at all. That is why the rule can refuse one and admit
// the other without a special case for either.
package bc_const_item_projection_return_fail;

struct In { a: i64 }
struct S  { a: i64, b: i64, i: In }

const K: S = S { a: 5i64, b: 6i64, i: In { a: 5i64 } };

fn f() -> &i64 { return &K.i.a; }

fn main() -> i32 { let p: &i64 = f(); return *p as i32; }
